\title{Project Report: Adapting ViT-Slimming to LLMs}
\author{Aamer Abdul
}
\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
\begin{quote}
Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated impressive performance across a wide range of natural language tasks, but their growing scale poses significant challenges in terms of inference latency and deployment cost. While existing acceleration techniques such as quantization, distillation, and unstructured pruning offer trade-offs between efficiency and accuracy, they often introduce implementation complexity or require access to pre-training data. In this work, we adapt the ViT-Slimming framework, originally developed for structured pruning of Vision Transformers for the efficient compression of LLMs through layer-wise pruning. Our approach applies differentiable, mask-based importance estimation to identify and remove unimportant transformer layers, significantly reducing model depth. To enable post pruning adaptation without retraining on the original pretraining dataset, we integrate Low Rank Adaptation (LoRA), allowing for lightweight finetuning on downstream post-training. Our method provides a simple and scalable alternative to existing LLM slimming techniques. Experimental results show that ViT-Slimming with LoRA can yield substantial inference speedups with minimal performance degradation, demonstrating its potential for practical LLM deployment in latency-sensitive applications.
\end{quote}
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Large Language Models (LLMs) such as GPT \cite
{brown2020language}, BERT \cite{devlin2018bert}, and LLaMA \cite{touvron2023llama} have revolutionized natural language processing by enabling general purpose language understanding and generation across a wide range of tasks. However, their increasing scale has led to growing concerns around inference latency, memory consumption, and deployment feasibility, particularly for real time applications or environments with constrained hardware. While several methods have been proposed to improve the efficiency of LLMs, such as model quantization \cite{gholami2021survey}, distillation \cite{sanh2019distilbert}, unstructured pruning \cite{frankle2018lottery}, and parameter efficient finetuning \cite{hu2021lora}, many of these approaches face significant limitations. For instance, unstructured pruning often leads to irregular memory access patterns that do not translate into real-world speedups, and distillation requires training and maintaining a separate smaller model.

Inspired by ViT-Slimming \cite{yang2022vit}, a model slimming framework originally proposed for Vision Transformers (ViTs), our project explores a novel approach to structured layer-wise pruning in LLMs. ViT-Slimming uses learnable soft masks and an $\ell_{1}$ sparsity constraint to identify and prune less important components across multiple architectural dimensions, such as attention heads and MLP widths, while preserving performance. Its one-shot search process and compatibility with pretrained weights make it a practical and efficient method for structured model compression. However, directly applying the original method to LLMs presents significant challenges. Most notably, the original framework involves retraining the slimmed model on the full pre-training dataset, which is computationally infeasible for large language models due to their size and data requirements.

In this project, we adapt ViT-Slimming to perform layer wise pruning of transformer blocks in LLMs, focusing solely on removing entire transformer layers to reduce model depth and improve inference latency. To make the approach practical, we replace full retraining with lightweight adaptation using Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) \cite{hu2021lora} in the post-training phase. This enables us to finetune the pruned model efficiently on downstream tasks without access to the original pretraining corpus. The motivation for this work lies in the need for practical and scalable model slimming techniques for LLMs that:

\begin{itemize}

\item Avoid full retraining on massive pretraining datasets.
\item Provide structured compression that translates to real latency gains.
\item Remain compatible with modern post-training techniques such as LoRA.
\item Can be applied with low overhead to existing pre-trained LLMs.

\end{itemize}

Compared to other LLM acceleration methods, our adapted ViT-Slim approach offers several key advantages. By focusing on structured pruning at the layer level, it produces models with which it is more straightforward to obtain speed ups in wall-clock inference times on standard hardware. Instead of relying on heuristics or manual design choices, our method uses a differentiable, mask-based search mechanism to learn, identify and prune the less important layers in the model. To make the process computationally feasible, we introduce a cost effective fine-tuning pipeline using LoRA \cite{hu2021lora}, which allows us to adapt the slimmed model to downstream tasks without retraining from scratch. Our findings suggest that combining ViT-Slimming principles with LoRA results in a practical and efficient strategy for reducing LLM inference latency with minimal performance degradation. This work lays the foundation for broader applications of structured slimming in LLMs and points toward a promising direction for resource efficient deployment.



\section{Related Works}

Previous pruning methods for LLMs primarily focus on compressing dense matrix weights \cite{ashkboos2024sparse}, attention heads \cite{michel2019sixteen}, MLP filters \cite{mccarley2019pruning}, or hidden dimensions \cite{xia2023llm}. While effective in reducing parameter count, these methods often introduce structural irregularities that limit compatibility with modern hardware and software deployment pipelines.

In contrast, layer pruning, which removes entire transformer blocks and reduces only model depth, offers a more structured and deployment-friendly form of compression. Several recent works have explored this direction, including LaCo \cite{yang2024laco}, ShortGPT \cite{men2024shortgpt}, UIDL \cite{gromov2024uidl}, SLEB \cite{song2024sleb}, and Shortened LLaMA \cite{kim2024shortened}. LaCo compresses groups of consecutive layers by averaging their weights into a single representative layer. ShortGPT introduces a BI score based on cosine similarity to assess and prune unimportant layers. UIDL similarly uses angular distance to rank and remove layers and relies on QLoRA for post-pruning healing. SLEB estimates layer importance using changes in perplexity, while Shortened LLaMA compares various pruning metrics and post-pruning strategies, including LoRA and continual pretraining.

Our work differs from these approaches by introducing a mask-based, differentiable search mechanism adapted from ViT-Slim \cite{yang2022vit} to identify unimportant layers. Rather than relying on hand-crafted scoring functions or layer-wise statistics, our method learns layer importance in a unified, data-driven manner during a single-shot training process. Additionally, we avoid full retraining by combining our pruning strategy with LoRA-based adaptation \cite{hu2021lora}, making our pipeline lightweight and practical for real-world LLM deployment.




\section{Methodology}

Recent large language models (LLMs) achieve state-of-the-art performance across a wide range of natural language understanding and generation tasks, but their sheer size introduces significant inference latency and resource bottlenecks. While many existing acceleration methods target unstructured weight pruning, quantization, or distillation, they often lack hardware efficiency or introduce complexity in training and deployment. Inspired by ViT-Slimming \cite{yang2022vit}, we explore a structured, interpretable approach to layer-wise pruning in LLMs.

Our key insight is that, unlike vision transformers where pruning multiple architectural dimensions (e.g., attention heads, MLP width, and patches) is common, pruning only entire transformer layers in LLMs can yield real latency reductions while preserving a simple model architecture. However, the original ViT-Slimming framework requires full retraining on the pretraining dataset—an infeasible step for LLMs. To overcome this, we introduce post-slimming adaptation using LoRA \cite{hu2021lora}, enabling efficient fine-tuning after pruning without retraining from scratch.

Our method answers the following questions:
\begin{itemize}
\item Can structured layer pruning be made feasible for LLMs without pretraining from scratch?
\item Can we leverage differentiable mask-based search to identify important layers automatically?
\item Can we maintain task performance while achieving real inference speedups?

\subsection{Layer-Wise Mask-Based Pruning}
We adopt the mask-based $l_{1}$ sparsity formulation from ViT-Slimming to identify less important layers in the LLM. Let a transformer model have $L$ encoder or decoder blocks, each composed of self-attention and feedforward modules. We define a learnable mask vector 
attention and feedforward modules. We define a learnable mask vector $z \in \mathbb{R}^L$, where each element $z_{\ell} \in$ $[0,1]$ controls the participation of the $\ell$-th layer.

During the forward pass, each layer's output is multiplied by its corresponding mask value:
$$
h_{\ell+1}=z_{\ell} \cdot \operatorname{Block}_{\ell}\left(h_{\ell}\right)
$$
where $h_{\ell}$ is the hidden representation at layer $\ell$.
The mask vector is optimized using a loss function combining the original task loss (e.g., cross-entropy on a downstream task) and an $\ell_1$ regularization term:
$$
\mathcal{L}_{\text {total }}=\mathcal{L}_{\text {task }}+\lambda\|z\|_1
$$

This encourages sparsity in $z$, effectively turning off less important layers during training. After optimization, we prune layers whose masks fall below a chosen threshold.



\subsection{Lightweight Adaptation via LoRA}

Pruning layers in a pretrained LLM can cause distributional shifts that degrade performance. Instead of retraining the pruned model on the original pretraining corpus, we apply Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) to the remaining layers. LoRA introduces trainable rank-decomposed matrices into attention and MLP layers, enabling low-cost fine-tuning:

$$
W^{\prime}=W+\Delta W=W+A B
$$
where $A \in R^{d \times r}, B \in R^{r \times d}$, and $r \ll d$.

This post-slimming adaptation phase allows the pruned model to recover performance on downstream tasks while keeping memory and computation overhead minimal.

\subsection{Practical Search and Deployment}

Our search procedure is performed directly on the frozen, pretrained model, eliminating the need for a supernet or full fine-tuning during the search phase. We jointly optimize the mask and LoRA parameters over a held-out downstream dataset, which ensures that layer importance is task-aware.

Once the pruning decision is finalized, we permanently remove masked layers, simplify the architecture, and optionally continue LoRA-based fine-tuning to close any performance gap. The final result is a structurally smaller model that achieves lower inference latency with minimal accuracy loss.



\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Layer-Wise Pruning of LLMs using ViT-Slimming and LoRA}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\REQUIRE Pretrained LLM $M$, downstream dataset $\mathcal{D}$, sparsity weight $\lambda$, pruning threshold $\tau$, LoRA config $\mathcal{C}_{\text{LoRA}}$
\ENSURE Slimmed model $M'$

\STATE Initialize scalar mask vector $z \in \mathbb{R}^L$ with $z_i = 1$ for each layer $i$
\STATE Inject $z$ as layer gates into model $M$
\STATE Apply LoRA adapters to model $M$ using config $\mathcal{C}_{\text{LoRA}}$

\FOR{each epoch}
    \FOR{each minibatch $(x, y)$ in $\mathcal{D}$}
        \STATE $y' \gets M(x)$ \COMMENT{Forward pass with masks and LoRA}
        \STATE $\mathcal{L}_{\text{task}} \gets \text{CrossEntropy}(y', y)$
        \STATE $\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}} \gets \mathcal{L}_{\text{task}} + \lambda \|z\|_1$
        \STATE Backpropagate and update $z$ and LoRA parameters
    \ENDFOR
\ENDFOR

\FOR{each layer $i$ in $M$}
    \IF{$z_i < \tau$}
        \STATE Remove layer $i$ from model $M$
    \ENDIF
\ENDFOR

\STATE Optionally finetune pruned model with LoRA on $\mathcal{D}$
\STATE \textbf{return} Pruned and adapted model $M'$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}



